# The warm server rows beside E21's public 3090 numbers (lcppwarm). E21's bench rows were already warm
# (e21ref.log: lcpp d6 21.36 / 21.82, d4096 20.83 / 20.88, timed 0..98), so this segment adds the server
# twin at the same placement: a check that llama-server's predicted_per_second and llama-bench's tg
# rate agree when both are warm, before the V4.1 A6000 table leans on the server rows alone.
#
# Wall [derived from e21ref.log]: ours 6 4096 at --place gate in one load of ~55 s, arm walls 3 / 20 s,
# each twice under PRIME: ~100 s a round; lcppsrv:6 lcppsrv:4096 at --n-cpu-moe 37 (the profile's
# LCPP_NCMOE when BLOOMERY_TIMING_GPU names the 3090, e21ref's lcpp arms)
# ~20 + 110 s a round; two rounds ~8.5 min, the two discards ~6 min (the lcpp one read cold, 230 s in
# e21ref), witnesses ~2 min: ~17 min.
kind: baseline
question: at warm engram rows, does llama-server's decode rate at depth 4096 on the RTX 3090 match
  llama-bench's warm row at the same placement?
term: tok/s @ n = 96, depth 4096, llama.cpp PR #28696 llama-server /completion predicted_per_second
  on the lcg ids after a same-id warm-up (-ngl 999 --n-cpu-moe 37 -fa on -t 32 --no-op-offload -fit
  off -np 1 -ctxcp 0 --cache-ram 0), RTX 3090 at 250 W, engine blocks with the discard
unit: tok/s
predict: 20.3..21.4
condition: one lease, two rounds, BLOOMERY_AB_ORDER=blocks, BLOOMERY_WARM_ROWS=1,
  BLOOMERY_GEN_PLACE=gate, the 3090 as BLOOMERY_TIMING_GPU, arms 6 4096 lcppsrv:6 lcppsrv:4096 (the
  profile's LCPP_NCMOE is 37 on the 3090, as e21ref's lcpp arms ran); each block's DISCARD r0 row present; every server
  row's `majflt N` near 0; no [cpu-busy] or [other-busy]; the full output to a file first
reason: the bench rows' 20.83..20.88 widened by +-2.5 % [derived: both are per-decode-step rates
  (lcpp-warm.sh's header: the server's first token is the prompt's, so 95 steps over their wall
  against the bench's 96), at positions shifted by one; the widening covers what only the server
  step runs, a top_k 1 sample and the slot loop's bookkeeping, which no measured number prices yet];
  why now: the V4.1 A6000 table moves to server rows and needs one
  card where both rulers read the same warm file
decide-in: take the server rows as llama.cpp's warm decode rows on both cards; E21's bench rows stand
decide-below: the server path costs a term the bench does not: name it from the server log (sampler,
  slot bookkeeping) before the V4.1 decode rows move to the server arm
decide-above: the bench's first-token term is larger than 1 %: re-derive the conversion in
  lcpp-warm.sh's header from this pair before any cross-ruler row is compared
